# Section 2: Introduction
lines.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
lines.append("")
lines.append('## 2. Introduction</summary><div class="details-content">')
lines.append("")
lines.append("Heart failure (HF) remains one of the greatest global healthcare challenges of the 21st century. Despite major advances in medical and device therapies, HF is associated with substantial mortality, frequent hospitalizations, and severe impairment of quality of life.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 2.1 Rationale for the 2026 Revision")
lines.append("Since the release of the 2021 ESC Guidelines for the diagnosis and treatment of acute and chronic heart failure and the 2023 Focused Update, transformative evidence has reshaped clinical practice:")
lines.append("1. **Therapeutic parity between mildly reduced and reduced LVEF:** Re-evaluations of the major neurohormonal trials (PARADIGM-HF, DAPA-HF, EMPEROR-Reduced, EMPEROR-Preserved, DELIVER) demonstrated consistent benefit across the ejection fraction continuum up to $50\\%$.")
lines.append("2. **Metabolic pharmacotherapies in HFpEF and obesity:** Landmark trials (EMPEROR-Preserved, DELIVER, STEP-HFpEF, SUMMIT) established SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 receptor agonists as disease-modifying therapies in HFpEF.")
lines.append("3. **Acute care transitions:** The terminology of \"acute heart failure\" has been replaced by Decompensated Heart Failure (DHF), emphasizing aggressive early decongestion, inpatient optimization of foundational therapies, and structured pre-discharge planning.")
lines.append("4. **Mechanical circulatory support trials:** The DanGer Shock trial demonstrated that microaxial flow pumps reduce 180-day mortality in STEMI-related cardiogenic shock, redefining the role of temporary MCS.")
lines.append("5. **Genetic and infiltrative cardiomyopathies:** Breakthrough amyloidosis therapies (tafamidis, vutrisiran, acoramidis) and expanding genetics require early systematic screening.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("</div></details>")
lines.append("")

# Section 3: Definition, Epidemiology, and Classifications
lines.append('<details class="med-details"><summary>')
lines.append("")
lines.append('## 3. Definition, Epidemiology, and Classifications</summary><div class="details-content">')
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 3.1 Definition of Heart Failure")
lines.append("Heart failure is a complex clinical syndrome comprising cardinal symptoms (breathlessness, fatigue, ankle swelling) and physical signs (elevated jugular venous pressure, pulmonary crackles, peripheral oedema) caused by structural and/or functional abnormalities of the heart, resulting in elevated intracardiac pressures and/or inadequate cardiac output at rest and/or during physical exertion.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("To establish a definitive diagnosis of heart failure, clinical symptoms must be corroborated by:")
lines.append("1. **Objective evidence of cardiac dysfunction:** Assessed primarily via transthoracic echocardiography (abnormal ejection fraction, chamber enlargement, diastolic dysfunction, significant valve disease).")
lines.append("2. **Elevated circulating biomarkers:** Natriuretic peptides (B-type natriuretic peptide [BNP] $\\ge 35\\text{ pg/mL}$ or N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide [NT-proBNP] $\\ge 125\\text{ pg/mL}$ in outpatients).")
lines.append("3. **Objective evidence of congestion:** Pulmonary or systemic congestion documented by chest radiography, lung ultrasound (B-lines), or invasive hemodynamic assessment.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("Asymptomatic individuals with cardiac dysfunction or elevated biomarkers do not have heart failure; they are classified as having **pre-heart failure (stage B)**.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 3.2 Epidemiology of Heart Failure")
lines.append("- **Prevalence:** Approximately $1\\%\\text{--}3\\%$ of the adult population in high-income nations, rising steeply to $> 10\\%$ in individuals aged $\\ge 70$ years.")
lines.append("- **Incidence:** Estimated at $1\\text{--}5$ cases per 1,000 person-years. While age-adjusted incidence of HFrEF has stabilized or slightly declined due to improved post-MI care, the incidence of HFpEF continues to escalate rapidly due to population aging and the obesity/metabolic syndrome epidemic.")
lines.append("- **Prognosis:** Despite therapeutic strides, 1-year and 5-year mortality following an initial diagnosis remain approximately $15\\%\\text{--}20\\%$ and $50\\%$, respectively. Hospitalization for decompensation marks a critical inflection point, carrying a $10\\%\\text{--}15\\%$ in-hospital mortality and a $25\\%\\text{--}30\\%$ 30-day readmission rate.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("### 3.3 Classifications of Heart Failure")
lines.append("")
lines.append("#### 3.3.1 Classification Based on Left Ventricular Ejection Fraction (LVEF)")
lines.append("The 2026 Guidelines introduce a modernized, binary ejection fraction framework:")
lines.append("")
lines.append("| Phenotype | LVEF Range | Key Diagnostic Criteria | Core Therapeutic Focus |")
lines.append("|---|---|---|---|")
lines.append("| **HFrEF** (Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction) | **$\\le 50\\%$** | Symptoms $\\pm$ signs; LVEF $\\le 50\\%$ on echocardiography | Universal Foundational Medical Therapy (**FMT quad-therapy**: ARNI/ACEi, beta-blocker, MRA, SGLT2i), loop diuretics for congestion, device therapy (ICD, CRT). |")
lines.append("| **HFpEF** (Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction) | **$> 50\\%$** | Symptoms $\\pm$ signs; LVEF $> 50\\%$; objective evidence of structural/functional abnormality (LVH, LAE, elevated $E/e'$) and/or elevated natriuretic peptides | **SGLT2 inhibitors** (Class I), GLP-1 RA for obesity, MRA/ARNI, loop diuretics, aggressive comorbidity management. |")
lines.append("")
lines.append("> [!NOTE]")
lines.append("> **Abolition of HFmrEF:** The previously designated intermediate category of \"heart failure with mildly reduced ejection fraction\" (LVEF $41\\text{--}49\\%$) has been merged into HFrEF. Clinical trials demonstrated identical response to foundational neurohormonal blockade in this group, eliminating artificial therapeutic barriers.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("#### 3.3.2 Classification Based on Heart Failure Stages")
lines.append("The ESC officially adopts the universal four-stage classification (A–D):")
lines.append("- **Stage A (At Risk):** Individuals at high risk for HF (hypertension, diabetes, ASCVD, obesity, cardiotoxin exposure) but without structural heart disease, elevated biomarkers, or symptoms.")
lines.append("- **Stage B (Pre-Heart Failure):** Asymptomatic individuals with structural heart disease (e.g., LVEF $\\le 50\\%$, LVH, regional wall motion abnormality, moderate/severe valvular disease), increased filling pressures, or elevated natriuretic peptides / troponins.")
lines.append("- **Stage C (Symptomatic Heart Failure):** Current or previous symptoms and signs caused by structural/functional cardiac abnormalities.")
lines.append("- **Stage D (Advanced Heart Failure):** Severe symptoms (NYHA III–IV) refractory to guideline-directed therapies, marked functional limitation, recurrent hospitalizations, and medication intolerance.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("![Figure 2: Heart failure stages and classifications](images/fig02_stages_classifications_hf.jpg)")
lines.append("")
lines.append("**Figure 2: Heart failure stages and classifications.** Progressive continuum from Stage A (at risk) through Stage B (pre-HF) to Stage C (symptomatic HF) and Stage D (advanced HF), illustrating clinical milestones, diagnostic biomarkers, and tailored therapeutic strategies.")
lines.append("")
lines.append("#### 3.3.3 New Nomenclature for Heart Failure Therapies")
lines.append("To foster structured prescribing and prevent clinical inertia, therapies are categorized into three hierarchical tiers:")
lines.append("1. **Foundational Medical Therapy (FMT):** Universal first-line \"quad-therapy\" proven to reduce mortality and hospitalizations in HFrEF:")
lines.append("   - Renin-Angiotensin System Inhibitor: Angiotensin Receptor–Neprilysin Inhibitor (**ARNI**) preferred, or ACE inhibitor (**ACE-I**), or ARB if intolerant.")
lines.append("   - Beta-Blocker: Evidence-based agents (**bisoprolol, carvedilol, metoprolol succinate**, nebivolol).")
lines.append("   - Mineralocorticoid Receptor Antagonist (**MRA**): Steroidal (spironolactone, eplerenone).")
lines.append("   - Sodium–Glucose Cotransporter-2 Inhibitor (**SGLT2-I**): **Dapagliflozin or empagliflozin**.")
lines.append("2. **Additional Medical Therapy (AMT):** Tailored add-on pharmacotherapies:")
lines.append("   - **Loop Diuretics:** Titrated to achieve and maintain euvolemia.")
lines.append("   - **GLP-1 Receptor Agonists (semaglutide, tirzepatide):** Indicated for HFpEF with obesity.")
lines.append("   - **Vericiguat:** Soluble guanylate cyclase stimulator for worsening HFrEF despite FMT.")
lines.append("   - **Ivabradine:** For sinus rhythm with resting HR $\\ge 70\\text{ b.p.m.}$ despite maximally tolerated beta-blockade.")
lines.append("   - **Cardiac Glycosides (digoxin, digitoxin):** In persistent symptoms or AF with rapid ventricular rate.")
